  "summary_text": "Kyle, yesterday was a mixed signal\u2014Port Washington fired on all cylinders while Hicksville remains severely understaffed. You should be proud of Port Washington's 78% revenue jump, but the real story is capacity: one active groomer produced $305 against a trailing-30-day baseline of $495. Today you're scheduling five groomers at Port Washington, and that's why you'll see the revenue scale. The concern is that MTD Port Washington is +15.7% against DOW-adjusted expectation, which is genuinely strong\u2014the team is executing\u2014but you're running at 2.0 groomer-days per day versus 5.5 last August. You're squeezing more per groomer, but you're undershooting the month because absolute capacity is down 64%. Add-on attach rate at Port Washington dropped to 29% from 50% week-over-year; that's a upsell discipline signal worth a conversation with the floor team.\n\nHicksville is the operational concern. Zero grooming revenue yesterday, zero appointments week-to-date, down 66% MTD despite being only 9.5% below DOW-adjusted expectation. You have 18 groomer-days scheduled across the next seven days versus Port Washington's 34, and Hicksville has zero bather hours scheduled all week. This is a staffing/scheduling structure problem, not a market problem. The lapse pool at Hicksville is $91K annual value\u2014smaller than Port Washington but meaningful. You need to decide whether Hicksville is a growth location that needs groomer investment or a maintenance operation.\n\nPort Washington add-on and retail upside is real; retail was +60% yesterday. Inventory shortages on high-velocity items (X-Small Treat Table at zero stock, Shanti's Medical Journey -47) are leaving money on the table at both locations.\n\n**Capacity & coverage: Port Washington today 5 groomers + 7.0h bathers, Hicksville 1 groomer + 0h bathers; next 7 days Port Washington 34 groomer-days, Hicksville 18 groomer-days.**"
